    {
      "title": "Part 4 — Managing temporary files",
      "content": [
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "17. What are temporary files?"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Main idea: Temporary files hold data that applications or services need for a short time."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Examples include:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/tmp — temporary data used by applications and users."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/run — runtime information used by services and other processes."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Much of /run is stored in memory. This is called volatile storage, meaning its contents are lost when the system reboots or loses power."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Linux must create required temporary directories when needed and remove old files so they do not waste storage or leave outdated information behind."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember: Temporary data helps applications work, but old data may need cleaning."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "18. What is systemd-tmpfiles?"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Main idea: systemd-tmpfiles creates, removes, and manages temporary files and directories according to configuration rules."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "When RHEL starts, an early service runs:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "systemd-tmpfiles --create --remove"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "systemd-tmpfiles — the management tool."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "--create — creates configured files and directories when needed."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "--remove — removes items marked for removal in the configuration."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "It reads configuration files from:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/run/tmpfiles.d/"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/etc/tmpfiles.d/"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Think of it as a maintenance worker following a written checklist."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember: The configuration defines what should be created, removed, or have its permissions corrected."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "19. Automatically cleaning temporary files"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Main idea: A systemd timer regularly triggers a service that removes temporary files that have exceeded their configured age."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The timer is:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "systemd-tmpfiles-clean.timer"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "It activates:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "systemd-tmpfiles-clean.service"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "That service runs:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "systemd-tmpfiles --clean"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "To inspect the timer configuration:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "systemctl cat systemd-tmpfiles-clean.timer"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Two important timer settings are:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "OnBootSec=15min"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "OnUnitActiveSec=1d"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "OnBootSec=15min — schedule the first activation 15 minutes after boot."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "OnUnitActiveSec=1d — schedule another activation one day after the associated unit was last activated."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Changing the interval to 30min makes subsequent activations occur every 30 minutes after the timer configuration is applied, subject to timer and service behavior."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember: The timer schedules cleanup; the service performs it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "20. Cleaning temporary files manually"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Main idea: You can run the cleanup command yourself instead of waiting for the timer."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "systemd-tmpfiles --clean"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The tool checks the configured age limits and removes eligible files. It does not automatically delete every temporary file."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For example, a configuration can specify that files older than one day should be removed during cleanup."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember: --clean applies the cleanup rules already defined in the configuration files."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "21. Understanding the seven tmpfiles fields"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Main idea: Each tmpfiles rule describes the action, path, permissions, ownership, age, and optional extra information."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The general format is:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Type Path Mode UID GID Age Argument"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Example:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "d /run/systemd/seats 0755 root root -"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Field"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Meaning"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "d"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Create the directory if it does not exist"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/run/systemd/seats"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Directory path"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "0755"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Permissions"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "root"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Owner"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "root"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Group"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "-"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "No age limit specified"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "No value"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "No additional argument"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "0755 means the owner can read, write, and enter the directory, while group members and others can read and enter it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "22. Important tmpfiles rule types"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Main idea: The type letter tells systemd-tmpfiles what action to perform."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Example A — Create a directory"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "d /run/systemd/seats 0755 root root -"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Creates the directory if necessary, with the specified ownership and permissions. This rule does not set an age-based cleanup limit."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Example B — Create and clean a directory"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "D /home/student 0700 student student 1d"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Creates the directory if needed and allows its contents to be cleaned according to the one-day age rule."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Example C — Create a symbolic link"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "L /run/fstablink - root root - /etc/fstab"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Creates /run/fstablink pointing to /etc/fstab. A symbolic link is like a shortcut to another file."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Example D — Restore security settings"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The type Z recursively restores SELinux contexts, file permissions, and ownership according to the rule."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember: The type determines the action; the remaining fields tell the tool where and how to apply it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "23. Configuration file precedence"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Main idea: If configuration files have the same name, the file in the higher-priority location takes precedence."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Location"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Purpose"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/etc/tmpfiles.d/"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Administrator settings"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/run/tmpfiles.d/"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Temporary runtime settings"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Package-provided defaults"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "The priority is:"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/etc/tmpfiles.d/ — highest"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/run/tmpfiles.d/"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "/usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/ — lowest"
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "For example, if a package provides a configuration file under /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/, an administrator can create a file with the same name under /etc/tmpfiles.d/ to override it."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Avoid editing package-provided files directly because package updates may replace them."
        },
        {
          "level": 0,
          "text": "Remember: Put your custom tmpfiles rules under /etc/tmpfiles.d/."
        }
      ],
      "words": 691
